                <item name="Name" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Panel</item>
              </items>
              <chunks count="1">
                <chunk name="Container">
                  <items count="8">
                    <item name="Description" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">A panel for custom notes and String values</item>
                    <item name="InstanceGuid" type_name="gh_guid" type_code="9">da60120e-ec49-4960-8b56-3bd5b00eabb6</item>
                    <item name="Name" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Panel</item>
                    <item name="NickName" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">PPU</item>
                    <item name="Optional" type_name="gh_bool" type_code="1">false</item>
                    <item name="ScrollRatio" type_name="gh_double" type_code="6">0</item>
                    <item name="SourceCount" type_name="gh_int32" type_code="3">0</item>
                    <item name="UserText" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">PPU = pixels per Rhino unit.

Let's say you want to draw a very complex drawing, consisting of thousands of instructions. You set the bitmap rectangle to 2000x2000 pixels. It takes ages (few seconds) to draw it, not really convienient if you want to make some adjustments.

Solution is to lower the PPU. If you lower it to 0.1, Squid will rescale the bitmap to 200x200 pixels. It will also TRY to rescale all the fills, line widths, dash patterns etc., thus preserving the overall appearance as much as possible.

It will draw the bitmap much, much faster. After you're done with your adjustments, you can set the PPU back to 1... or even increase it.</item>
                  </items>
